{"role":"assistant","content":"Fixed and verified. Three separate defects, each mapping to one symptom in `logs/`:\n\n## Causes (full write-up in `ANSWER.md`)\n\n1. **Connect button 401 for everyone** — the button was a plain `<a href=\"/api/intervals/auth/init\">`. The sign-in token only travels as an `Authorization: Bearer` header added by the SPA's fetch wrapper; browsers never attach it to link navigations, so every click hit the endpoint with no credentials (the dev-tools capture in `connect-attempt.log` confirms a headerless document navigation).\n\n2. **Authorize step 400 `invalid_request`** — `auth_init` never sent `response_type=code`, which RFC 6749 makes mandatory. Intervals.icu used to tolerate the omission (September worked with this code), then started enforcing it. The support ticket's calendar-scope theory is a red herring — `CALENDAR:READ/WRITE` are valid scopes per Intervals.icu's OAuth docs; the calendar sync just shipped when the provider tightened validation.\n\n3. **Connection \"expires\" after an hour** — Intervals.icu's token response has no `expires_in` and no `refresh_token` (their documented response shape, matching Rivera's log exactly; its tokens don't expire). The server invented a 1-hour TTL and killed the connection when it passed, leaving Rivera stranded since the reconnect button was broken by cause 1.\n\n## Fixes\n\n- `server.py` — `auth_init` sends `response_type=code` and returns the authorize URL as JSON to an authenticated fetch; `store_connection` stores a token as non-expiring when the provider omits `expires_in`; the refresher (extracted to `refresh_due`) skips non-expiring tokens but still renews/expires `expires_in`-bearing ones.\n- `static/app.js` — the Connect control is now a button that fetches `/api/intervals/auth/init` via the `api()` wrapper, then navigates to the returned URL.\n\n## Verification (offline)\n\nUsing a temporary local mock of Intervals.icu via the `INTERVALS_BASE` override (deleted afterwards): full connect flow for both `tok-okafor` and `tok-rivera` (auth/init → consent redirect → callback → `status: connected` → wellness data returns); the old URL shape still reproduces the 400 while the new one passes; a refresher pass simulated 2 hours after connect leaves the Intervals-shaped connection connected, while `expires_in`-bearing connections still expire/refresh as before. `node --check` passes on the SPA.\n\nTwo things worth knowing:\n- **`auth/init`'s contract changed** from `302` to `200 {\"url\": ...}` — the curl paste-the-link workflow is now `curl -s -H \"Authorization: Bearer <token>\" .../api/intervals/auth/init` and open the returned `url`.\n- Port 8766 was held by a stale dev server from another copy of this workspace (`/private/tmp/h-hszrJe`); I killed it to run verification — no data loss, its store was in-memory."}
